      "text": "To determine the measure of angle \\( P \\) in the given polygon, we can use the fact that the sum of the interior angles of a polygon is given by the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Sum of interior angles} = (n-2) \\times 180^\\circ\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( n \\) is the number of sides of the polygon. However, we need to find the measure of angle \\( P \\) given the measures of the other angles in the polygon.\n\nLet's denote the measures of the angles at vertices \\( A \\), \\( B \\), \\( C \\), and \\( D \\) as \\( \\angle A = 128^\\circ \\), \\( \\angle B = 92^\\circ \\), \\( \\angle C = 113^\\circ \\), and \\( \\angle D = 111^\\circ \\), and the measure of angle \\( P \\) as \\( \\angle P \\).\n\nSince the sum of the interior angles of the polygon is \\( 540^\\circ \\) (as the polygon has 4 sides), we can write:\n\n\\[\n\\angle A + \\angle B + \\angle C + \\angle D + \\angle P = 540^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the given values:\n\n\\[\n128^\\circ + 92^\\circ + 113^\\circ + 111^\\circ + \\angle P = 540^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the left side:\n\n\\[\n444^\\circ + \\angle P = 540^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( \\angle P \\):\n\n\\[\n\\angle P = 540^\\circ - 444^\\circ = 96^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThus, the measure of angle \\( P \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{96}\n\\]",
