      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the measure of angle \\( P \\) in the given geometric configuration, we need to analyze the relationships between the angles in the pentagon formed by the points \\( A, B, C, D, \\) and \\( E \\).\n\nGiven:\n- \\(\\angle PAB = 128^\\circ\\)\n- \\(\\angle ACB = 92^\\circ\\)\n- \\(\\angle AED = 113^\\circ\\)\n- \\(\\angle ADE = 111^\\circ\\)\n\nFirst, let's find the measure of \\(\\angle BAC\\). Since \\(\\angle BAC\\) and \\(\\angle ACB\\) form a straight line, we have:\n\\[\n\\angle BAC = 180^\\circ - \\angle ACB = 180^\\circ - 92^\\circ = 88^\\circ\n\\]\n\nNext, we know that \\(\\angle ADE\\) and \\(\\angle AED\\) are supplementary angles (they form a straight line with \\(\\angle AED\\)). Therefore:\n\\[\n\\angle ADE + \\angle AED = 180^\\circ\n\\]\nSubstituting the given value of \\(\\angle AED\\):\n\\[\n\\angle ADE + 113^\\circ = 180^\\circ\n\\]\nSolving for \\(\\angle ADE\\):\n\\[\n\\angle ADE = 180^\\circ - 113^\\circ = 67^\\circ\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the measure of \\(\\angle P\\). Notice that \\(\\angle P\\) is part of the larger angle \\(\\angle BAE\\) at vertex \\( A \\). To find \\(\\angle BAE\\), we can use the fact that the sum of the interior angles of a pentagon is \\(540^\\circ\\). However, we can also use the fact that \\(\\angle P\\) is composed of the angles around point \\( A \\):\n\n\\[\n\\angle P = \\angle BAC + \\angle BAP + \\angle AEP\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\angle BAP\\) is part of \\(\\angle BAC\\) and \\(\\angle AEP\\) is part of the sum of the remaining angles around point \\( A \\). We can use the fact that the sum of the angles around point \\( A \\) is \\(180^\\circ\\).\n\nGiven the angles we have:\n\\[\n\\angle BAC = 88^\\circ, \\quad \\angle ACB = 92^\\circ, \\quad \\angle AED = 113^\\circ, \\quad \\angle ADE = 67^\\circ\n\\]\n\nWe need to find \\(\\angle BAP\\) and \\(\\angle AEP\\). However, we can directly use the fact that the sum of the angles around point \\( A \\) is \\(180^\\circ\\).\n\n\\[\n\\angle P = \\angle BAC + \\angle BAP + \\angle AEP\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\angle BAP\\) and \\(\\angle AEP\\) are not directly given, we can use the fact that the sum of the angles in the pentagon is \\(540^\\circ\\).\n\n\\[\n\\angle BAC + \\angle ABC + \\angle BCD + \\angle CDE + \\angle DEA = 540^\\circ\n\\]\n\nGiven the angles:\n\\[\n88^\\circ + \\angle ABC + \\angle BCD + \\angle CDE + 67^\\circ = 540^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(\\angle ABC\\), \\(\\angle BCD\\), and \\(\\angle CDE\\):\n\\[\n88^\\circ + \\angle ABC + \\angle BCD + \\angle CDE + 67^\\circ = 540^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\n155^\\circ + \\angle ABC + \\angle BCD + \\angle CDE = 540^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\n\\angle ABC + \\angle BCD + \\angle CDE = 385^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\angle P\\) is part of this sum, we need to find the correct combination. Given the angles around \\( A \\), we can find:\n\\[\n\\angle P = \\angle BAC + \\angle BAP + \\angle AEP\n\\]\n\nAfter re-evaluating the angles:\n\\[\n\\angle P = 128^\\circ + 113^\\circ + 67^\\circ = 308^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThus, the measure of angle \\( P \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{52^\\circ}\n\\]",
